    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cash transfer payments may affect migration pattens and unemployment in rural\r\nareas where poverty is widespread. To characterize those effects, I examine trends in\r\nmigration and unemployment in Alaska in relation to variation in the amount of the\r\nAlaska Permanent Fund Dividend (PFD). The PFD is an annual government cash\r\ntransfer guaranteed to nearly all Alaska residents; its amount is based on a five-year\r\naverage of the investment earnings of a public endowment. Using the identification\r\nassumption that the PFD amount is not well-correlated with other determinants of\r\nmigration or unemployment in Alaska, I show that larger cash transfers are positively\r\nrelated to migration rates from remote rural areas to urban areas like Anchorage,\r\nAlaska’s largest city. I also show that larger cash transfers are negatively related to the unemployment rate in remote rural areas. These results are statistically significant and robust. Excluding the portion of the PFD that is attributable to the investment earnings of the Alaska Permanent Fund in the most recent fiscal years does not produce significantly different results, suggesting that the identification assumption is wellfounded. I discuss consequences of the migration effect and propose policy responses.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
